From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 08:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rocket: reset the NPU before detaching its IOMMU domain

rocket_reset() detaches the core's IOMMU group and only then asserts the
core's resets.  It is entered because the core is wedged -- the one
caller that a client can reach is rocket_job_timedout() -- so the detach
runs against an NPU that is still mid-DMA, with an unacknowledged fault
sitting in its MMU.

rk_iommu cannot stall a bank in that state.  Detaching therefore ends in

  rk_iommu fdab9000.iommu: Enable stall request timed out, status: 0x00001d
  rk_iommu fdab9000.iommu: Enable stall request timed out, status: 0x00018b

one dev_err per MMU bank, on every faulting job.  Those come from
rk_iommu_disable(), which ignores the error and carries on -- but the
same handshake runs again inside rk_iommu_enable() when the IOMMU core
puts the group back on its default domain, and there its failure is
returned.  __iommu_group_set_core_domain() turns that into

  WARNING: drivers/iommu/iommu.c at __iommu_group_set_core_domain
   iommu_detach_group / rocket_reset.part.0 [rocket] / rocket_job_timedout

which is a taint and a backtrace, and a panic on a kernel booted with
panic_on_warn.  drm_rocket_task.regcmd is a raw NPU IOVA that
rocket_job_hw_submit() writes into the PC block unvalidated, and
/dev/accel/accel0 is group `render`, so an unprivileged client reaches
this by pointing one field at an unmapped address.

Reset the core first.  The reset quiesces the master and wipes the MMU
page-table base -- which is already why the domain has to be dropped at
all -- so by the time the group is detached the handshake has something
it can stall, and it completes.

The detach moves out of the job_lock scope with it.  That lock covers
in_flight_job, which the IRQ path also touches; core->attached_domain is
written only from the drm_sched run/reset path, and drm_sched_stop()
above has already fenced off run_job for this core (the serialisation
083 documents at the other write site).

VERIFIED ON RK3588 SILICON (Turing RK1, 7.1.1, rocket 081-087 built
out-of-tree).  Thirty client-requested DMA faults per arm --
tests/uapi_regcmd_fault_rocket in both its read and write modes, fifteen
rounds each, over two independent A/B rounds -- give thirty "NPU job
timed out" on either module.  On the unpatched one they also give **60
stall timeouts**, exactly two per fault, one per MMU bank; on the
patched one, **zero**, with the thirty job timeouts unchanged.  83 of 83
ctest entries pass on the patched module and the taint word does not
move.

The WARN itself is intermittent -- it needs the second handshake, the
one behind the default domain, to fail as well -- and it did not fire in
either arm of this A/B.  What is measured here is the failing handshake
it is downstream of, which this removes outright.

rocket_core_fini() detaches without a reset too, but that path tears the
core down after rocket_job_fini() has drained the scheduler, so there is
no wedged job to stall behind and it is left alone.

This is mainline code.  The RK3576 reaches the same detach and produces
the same rk_iommu stall lines, but not the WARNING -- the two parts
differ in the consequence rather than in the trigger -- so this is
carried as an RK3588 patch and the RK3576 series does not need it.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 260285bcc..67e4f48a5 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -469,23 +469,31 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 		if (core->in_flight_job)
 			pm_runtime_put_noidle(core->dev);
 
-		/*
-		 * The reset wipes the IOMMU page-table base, so drop the
-		 * attached domain to force the next job to re-attach and
-		 * reprogram it.
-		 */
-		if (core->attached_domain) {
-			iommu_detach_group(core->attached_domain->domain, core->iommu_group);
-			rocket_iommu_domain_put(core->attached_domain);
-			core->attached_domain = NULL;
-		}
-
 		core->in_flight_job = NULL;
 	}
 
-	/* Proceed with reset now. */
+	/*
+	 * Reset the NPU before touching its IOMMU. A reset is entered because
+	 * the core is wedged -- typically mid-DMA -- and detaching the group
+	 * while it still is makes the rk_iommu stall handshake time out, which
+	 * is both a dev_err per bank and, when the force-reset behind the
+	 * default domain fails too, a WARN in the IOMMU core reachable by any
+	 * client of /dev/accel.
+	 */
 	rocket_core_reset(core);
 
+	/*
+	 * The reset wiped the IOMMU page-table base, so drop the attached
+	 * domain to force the next job to re-attach and reprogram it. Safe
+	 * outside job_lock: attached_domain is written only from the
+	 * drm_sched run/reset path, which drm_sched_stop() above has fenced.
+	 */
+	if (core->attached_domain) {
+		iommu_detach_group(core->attached_domain->domain, core->iommu_group);
+		rocket_iommu_domain_put(core->attached_domain);
+		core->attached_domain = NULL;
+	}
+
 	/* NPU has been reset, we can clear the reset pending bit. */
 	atomic_set(&core->reset.pending, 0);
 
-- 
